00:17:58,711 --> 00:18:01,190
They had withdrawable money.

254
00:18:01,190 --> 00:18:03,350
They had deposit money.

255
00:18:03,350 --> 00:18:08,390
They had free spin money and then they had rules for how to convert them to each other.

256
00:18:08,390 --> 00:18:13,909
So there's a good example where they actively had made a modeling decision to separate different things.

257
00:18:13,919 --> 00:18:21,110
I think it all comes down to if you are careful and start modeling your concepts.

258
00:18:21,120 --> 00:18:27,510
And add how should I say specific rules to it.

259
00:18:27,510 --> 00:18:31,029
Then it becomes much much harder to inject something that violates that.

260
00:18:31,039 --> 00:18:37,111
Like we see for every injection attack that you inject something that the software didn't expect. Right?

261
00:18:37,111 --> 00:18:44,232
So you can access the database and it behaves in a way you didn't anticipate and so forth, right? So yeah, be specific.

262
00:18:44,240 --> 00:18:48,630
That's our rule. Either inject or to confuse the state.

263
00:18:48,640 --> 00:18:51,430
Yeah. That you end up in a state.

264
00:18:51,430 --> 00:18:54,950
that was not anticipated and not desired.

265
00:18:54,950 --> 00:18:59,830
but enabled the user to do stuff that they shouldn't be able to do.

266
00:18:59,830 --> 00:19:03,750
We've been talking around the idea of domain objects which I don't think we actually specified.

