00:21:05,919 --> 00:21:07,750
Absolutely.

292
00:21:07,750 --> 00:21:10,789
A value object that is not immutable would be a very strange thing.

293
00:21:10,799 --> 00:21:13,990
The next questions I have are around validations.

294
00:21:13,990 --> 00:21:15,990
But something occurred to me while you were talking,

295
00:21:15,990 --> 00:21:19,909
Daniel, about room numbers and oh, I want to charge something to my room.

296
00:21:19,909 --> 00:21:23,430
There seems to me there actually are two validations there.

297
00:21:23,430 --> 00:21:25,590
One is that it's actually a valid room number,

298
00:21:25,590 --> 00:21:27,590
but the other one is that there's actually somebody staying in that room.

299
00:21:27,600 --> 00:21:33,909
Yes, exactly. And so in the book, y'all make a very strong point about the order in.

300
00:21:33,909 --> 00:21:38,870
which you validate stuff and how that contributes to security. Can you talk a little bit about that?

301
00:21:38,880 --> 00:21:41,430
Yeah.

302
00:21:41,430 --> 00:21:43,110
So the general principle here is that.

303
00:21:43,110 --> 00:21:48,310
if you look at the relationship between an attacker and the attackee I guess.

304
00:21:48,310 --> 00:21:50,549
Then you want the relationship to be asymmetric in the sense.

305
00:21:50,549 --> 00:21:55,590
That it should be really expensive for the attacker to craft the malicious input.

306
00:21:55,600 --> 00:22:00,149
And it should be really cheap for the the recipient the server right.

307
00:22:00,149 --> 00:22:05,029
So we should be able to reject something quickly and at a low cost.

308
00:22:05,029 --> 00:22:10,310
So because of that we've just created what we call an order of validation.

309
00:22:10,310 --> 00:22:15,590
Basically it's a five-step order where we do the cheapest operations first.

310
00:22:15,590 --> 00:22:19,830
and well the first operation to check that would guess be origin.

311
00:22:19,830 --> 00:22:22,470
Does this come from a valid place.

312
00:22:22,470 --> 00:22:24,390
It could be that it does come from something that you trust.

313
00:22:24,390 --> 00:22:26,230
Or that it has correct token or something like that.

314
00:22:26,240 --> 00:22:30,310
That it's it's actually comes from a valid origin.

315
00:22:30,310 --> 00:22:32,310
It's okay to have data from that point.

316
00:22:32,310 --> 00:22:38,549
The second thing to check would be size or length stuff like that.

317
00:22:38,549 --> 00:22:42,549
That would be constant execution time.

318
00:22:42,549 --> 00:22:47,029
Basically very cheap to validate is this room number indeed of proper length.

319
00:22:47,039 --> 00:22:53,510
and it might be just roughly like order of magnitude.

320
00:22:53,510 --> 00:22:59,430
If we expect something of 100 bytes, well check that it's no more than thousand bytes.

321
00:22:59,430 --> 00:23:04,549
So that means that what you have successfully done simply by doing.

322
00:23:04,549 --> 00:23:08,310
that you have limit input vectors to meet that requirement.

323
00:23:08,310 --> 00:23:10,549
They can't be larger than your limit.

324
00:23:10,549 --> 00:23:17,270
They can't be longer than your if it is a string can't be more characters that you expect.

325
00:23:17,270 --> 00:23:20,070
Or it can't be bigger in size if it is kilobytes or bytes or something.

326
00:23:20,080 --> 00:23:22,070
The second thing that you do if it fits.

327
00:23:22,080 --> 00:23:27,590
that criterion you have to invest a little bit more.

328
00:23:27,590 --> 00:23:30,230
So in this case we want to see.

329
00:23:30,230 --> 00:23:34,230
that you know what does this input contain the proper characters.

330
00:23:34,230 --> 00:23:39,590
We don't care about the order we care about it is the valid characters.

331
00:23:39,590 --> 00:23:42,549
And in a room number it might be just letters.

332
00:23:42,549 --> 00:23:45,590
It could be a specific subset of letters can say.

333
00:23:45,600 --> 00:23:50,870
that oh a room number could either start with a or b but no other character.

334
00:23:50,870 --> 00:23:53,590
And then there's going to be four digits.

335
00:23:53,590 --> 00:24:01,511
So you would know that oh it can contain a or b or the digits 0 to 9. That's it.

336
00:24:01,511 --> 00:24:03,430
So you don't care which order it is,

337
00:24:03,430 --> 00:24:10,710
but you say this input data does indeed contain the proper characters.

338
00:24:10,710 --> 00:24:13,190
But what we do at that point is.

339
00:24:13,190 --> 00:24:15,750
that we actually open up the package and look inside it.

340
00:24:15,760 --> 00:24:17,990
And that's why the first two origin.

341
00:24:17,990 --> 00:24:27,191
and size are so powerful because you can throw away a lot of attacks without even looking on. the inside.

342
00:24:27,191 --> 00:24:31,029
So the idea here is that we have to invest a little bit like Dan said.

343
00:24:31,029 --> 00:24:33,670
We have to open up the package. We have to look at the content.

344
00:24:33,679 --> 00:24:39,430
and if the characters match then we say well the likelihood now.

345
00:24:39,430 --> 00:24:43,510
that it is indeed a room number is quite high.

346
00:24:43,510 --> 00:24:48,710
But there is a chance that somebody injected something.

347
00:24:48,710 --> 00:24:53,430
that it contains only A or B at the end or in the middle or only characters.

348
00:24:53,430 --> 00:24:58,549
So we need to make sure that the order of the characters are also indeed correct.

349
00:24:58,549 --> 00:25:03,590
And in that sense we would have to parse the data like we could have a JSON.

350
00:25:03,590 --> 00:25:08,390
But we need to check that it's a well-formed JSON and that it's not incredibly deep.

351
00:25:08,400 --> 00:25:10,549
Yeah.

352
00:25:10,549 --> 00:25:12,149
So doing that we will then of course send.

353
00:25:12,149 --> 00:25:14,549
that data to the could be a regular expression engine checking that.

354
00:25:14,559 --> 00:25:16,630
And the reason for why we're doing this.

355
00:25:16,640 --> 00:25:19,990
so late in the order of validation is.

356
00:25:20,000 --> 00:25:26,630
because the reg engine the backtracking algorithm that's used in there.

357
00:25:26,630 --> 00:25:30,230
Is susceptible to bad input that could sort of halt your system.

358
00:25:30,240 --> 00:25:34,710
And that is of course goes for a JSON parser.

359
00:25:34,710 --> 00:25:37,750
and XML parser as well classical things you try to attack if you want to DDo someone.

360
00:25:37,760 --> 00:25:46,070
So basically we move up the ladder of both complexity and cost from a recipient's point of view.

361
00:25:46,070 --> 00:25:51,510
And if it passes all that then we can say yes this is indeed a true room number.

362
00:25:51,510 --> 00:25:54,149
in this case.

363
00:25:54,149 --> 00:25:56,070
And we can then instantiate a domain object for that.

364
00:25:56,080 --> 00:25:58,710
And then we end up at the fifth step.

365
00:25:58,720 --> 00:26:03,669
which I think you alluded to in the question like touched one.

366
00:26:03,669 --> 00:26:09,029
Which is now that we got this input does it make sense in the system at this point.

367
00:26:09,029 --> 00:26:12,950
of time.

368
00:26:12,950 --> 00:26:16,789
And to continue Daniel's explanation of what valuable resources we protect.

369
00:26:16,789 --> 00:26:18,950
Then what we're protecting here is of course the database.

370
00:26:18,950 --> 00:26:25,430
because to check whether something makes sense at this point of time we probably have to fetch some.

371
00:26:25,430 --> 00:26:28,390
data from the database which is pretty expensive is operation.

372
00:26:28,400 --> 00:26:36,950
so this fifth step is actually something we call the semantic validation or it's a semantical check.

